date: 2026-09-10; host Darwin 25.5.0 aarch64 (mac) for the encode-level numbers,
  four hosts for the kernel numbers (`cbrt_candidates_2026-09-10.*`).

WHAT SHIPPED: the forward XYB cube root is now SELECTED, not fixed.
  * `EncoderStrategy::Libjxl` -> `XybCubeRoot::Libjxl`, a transcription of
    libjxl `base/fast_math-inl.h::CubeRootAndAdd`. Bit-exactness with the
    reference is the point of that strategy.
  * zen strategies -> `XybCubeRoot::LowP` (`magetypes::cbrt_lowp`), named once
    as `vardct::xyb::ZEN_XYB_CBRT`.
  Gate `xyb_cbrt_libjxl_parity`, Section D. Verified LIVE, not assumed: the two
  modes produce different bytes on all four probed cells, so the selector really
  reaches the kernel (the LZ77-hash null of 2026-09-08 is the reason this check
  is mandatory).

The replaced implementation ran two Newton iterations in **f64**, each with a
DIVISION, and extracted every lane to scalar for its bit-hack guess. It was
neither libjxl's algorithm nor fast.

WHY LowP. Measured through the REAL DISPATCHER (not the isolated cbrt bench),
1 Mpx on aarch64, arms alternated:

  dispatch, LowP    1.31 ms/Mpx      <- shipped for zen
  dispatch, Libjxl  1.71 ms/Mpx      <- shipped for EncoderStrategy::Libjxl
  dispatch, MidP    1.89 ms/Mpx

LowP is 24 % cheaper than the next. Note the ordering is NOT the same as the
isolated-cbrt bench, where `cbrt_midp` and libjxl's were within a few percent:
in the real kernel, magetypes' `cbrt_midp`/`cbrt_lowp` still do their bit-hack
guess through `to_array()` / `from_array`, while the libjxl transcription stays
in integer vector lanes — which is why libjxl's beats MidP here and did not
there. Measure the kernel you ship, not the primitive.

The objection to LowP is accuracy — 259 ULP against MidP's 3 — so that was
CHECKED, on 60 lossy cells spanning all 21 imazen-26 strata at e{3,5,7} x
d{1,4}, LowP against the libjxl cube root:

  SSIM2 delta   min -0.0517   median +0.0005   max +0.1886
  bytes delta   min -1.240 %  median  0.000 %  max +0.234 %

Worst cell -0.0517 SSIM2 (a NOAA document scan at e7) — an order of magnitude
inside the ±0.30 SSIM2 per-cell budget this repo holds elsewhere (issue #25).
`just rd-regression` is green on ALL THREE variants with per-image SSIM2
differing by <= 0.09. magetypes documents `cbrt_lowp` as intended for exactly
this use: "perceptual colour (Oklab/XYB) targeting 8-bit output".

Corpus byte impact of the whole change vs the pre-session build, 42 images x
e{3,5,7}: **median +0.000 %, mean -0.010 %, range -0.299 % .. +0.141 %.**

ENCODE WALL, new / pre-session build (9 reps, 5 images, arms alternated):
  e3 t1 0.919x   e3 t8 0.979x
  e5 t1 0.979x   e5 t8 0.995x
  e7 t1 0.988x   e7 t8 0.997x
e3 gains most because the XYB transform is the largest single share of its CPU
(15.3 %); the perceptual loop dominates e8+, so the cube root barely shows there.

SIDE EFFECT WORTH MORE THAN THE SPEED: every `XybCubeRoot` variant is pure f32,
so the kernel no longer needs `f64x4` — and **the `v4`/AVX-512 tier is back in
its `incant!` list**, having been excluded precisely because magetypes has no
`F64x4Backend` for `X64V4Token`. Not measured on an AVX-512 host in this change.

BYTES: 29 of 63 hash-lock cells moved, ALL lossy (lossless never touches XYB —
a correctness signal, not a coincidence). 0 cells added or removed. The largest
move is +224 B on a 79 KB synthetic-noise fixture (+0.28 %); on real content the
corpus median is 0.000 %. The Libjxl byte lock (5 cells) did NOT move.

VERIFIED: full release suite (1586 + 507 + ...), `--features "__expert
__internals"` it-suite 527, workspace all-targets clippy, divergence drift 7/7
(new Section D row + `extract_anchors` fallback), djxl decode 20/20 incl. 1x1 and
259x133, thread determinism 0 mismatches over t {1,3,8}.

TRAPS HIT: (1) `UPDATE_HASHES=1` without `rm -f` APPENDS a second full set, and
without `--test-threads=1` it produces a TORN file with interleaved half-lines.
`just update-hashes` does both correctly — use it. (2) The dispatch-parity test
now loops over ALL THREE cube roots; a tier that agreed on `MidP` but not
`Libjxl` would otherwise have shipped silently to Libjxl-strategy users only.
